  "strategy": "Approach and rationale: We need to find a function h(x) that minimizes the maximum overlap coefficient C5. The previous implementation is based on gradient-free optimization methods and random mutations, but local optimization might not be sufficient in complex landscapes. A Bayesian optimization method powered by Gaussian processes would use historical data to guide the search for better solutions. This approach sustains the feasibility of the solution while ensuring exploration of promising regions.\n\nMathematical specification: We formulate the solution as a constrained optimization problem where:\n- Decision variable: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all grid points\n- Constraint: \u222b_[0,2] h(x) dx = 1\n- Objective: Minimize max_k \u222b_[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nWe approximate the continuous integral as a discrete sum over grid points with step dx=2/n_points.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Central Gaussian Process Regression:\n   - Maintain a gp model (num_cols: GPRegressor) from past evaluation results\n   - Collect trials (h_vectors) and scores (C5 values)\n   - Score_candidates(h) estimates C5 without costly evaluation\n   - Experimental design: Use EI pseud-gradient for candidate selection\n\n2. Generator Circuit:\n   - Bayesian-HillClimber hybrid\n   - Candidate families:\n        - Repair(parent) scaled to keep sum()\n        - Cyclical shifts\n        - Filter-domain filtering (Fourier)\n        - Local perturbations\n\n3. Dynamic dimensions:\n   - Base resolution n0=140 (provided)\n   - Dimensions = [n0, 2*n0, 3*n0] up to budget\n   - Days/hr limit based on budget value\n\n4. Execution loop:\n   - Repeat( until time or iteration cap):\n        candidates = []\n        EI.maximize2d_grid( gp_model ) -> idx\n        conduct EI trial(h[idx])\n        if new solution improves global best:\n            best_h = trial\n            save_gp_candidates( best_h )\n            replenish budget accordingly\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Cyclic domain stationary points: [0.5, grid_width/2]\n- Local repair threshold: scaling by desired feasibility\n- Noise robustness: large neighborhood safe zones\n- Gaussian process architecture: skopt.gpr or scikit-learn\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Lower dim = faster evaluation\n- Initial design: 140-point grid\n- Time check: every SLSQP trial under budget\n- Bayesian safety margin: sqrt(5)*lazy_pert_steps\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Must return C5 score reduced below 0.3808\n2. Repair( h, n ) to ensure sum(h)=n/2.0\n3. Pre-validation: Bayesian-scorensor passes \"sanity\"\n4. Preserve the correlation function as defined",
